A||||16||Jncomutt||84 2M4 - Full Tank Total Distance???||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||10-25-2001||05:37 PM||||I've noticed with my 2M4, My milage to a tank seems rather low.  When I completly top off the tank (slow speed fill up), I only seem to get about 160-170 miles before it will be in the Red.<P>Car has new plugs, new wires, new o2 sensor, MSD 6A ignition, MSD GM Coil, new rotor and distrib. cap, K&N air filter and I usually run it with 87 Octane gas.<P>The car has only 55k miles on it and seems to run fine otherwise.  Perhaps it has a Vac leak for I hear a slight whistle? maybe at around 4k RPM.  (I don't know if its just the engine sound)<P>Is this average or 'the right' miles to a tank for an 84?  Or anything else I should check/replace and see if my MPT increses?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jake_Dragon||10-25-2001||05:47 PM||||I get about the same, my Duke has 100k. I noticed that the exhaust tip is sooty. Other wise it runs ok for what I use it for.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jncomutt||10-25-2001||05:51 PM||||Hmm... going through the archives, I realize 'TheOgre' mentioned to try not topping off the tank but rather letting the pump cut it off.  I will try, but anyone have an Idea???<P>Oh yea, and I don't notice any soot...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-25-2001||06:24 PM||||Sounds like driving technique.  You should be getting 30+ mpg, unless you are driving with a lead foot.  At 160 mile between fillups, you are doing no better than 18-20 mpg.||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnt671||10-25-2001||06:32 PM||||I get between 200 to 220 miles to a tank of mixed driving depending on how I drive.||||reg||1
Z||000005||watts||10-25-2001||06:39 PM||||On my 87' 4cyl 5spd (keep in mind I have a bigger tank being a later model) I get about 400km's (250 miles) in the city, and about 550km's (340 miles) on the highway.<P>On any later model car with a charcoal canister you shouldn't 'top off' the tank.  You can have raw fuel drool back down the vent tube into the canister.  Make's things stink, and can wreck the canister.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by watts (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jncomutt||10-25-2001||06:47 PM||||I understand its about 20... I drive normal, any ideas why its this low???||||reg||1
Z||000007||kiteman||10-25-2001||09:17 PM||||I get 30 mpg with an 84 4 speed.  I generally let it go from full to about 1/8 tank and it gets about 210 -220 miles.<P>The reason you should avoid topping off is that with an extremely full tank you can get liquid into the gas vapor canister.  If that happens the charcoal gets pretty nasty.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SLYGUY||10-26-2001||03:49 AM||||I've also heard that it is much easier for the fuel pump to draw fuel from the tank when it's full. The more fuel is drawn from the tank, the more difficult the job gets for the pump. <BR>I always fill up at either 1/2 tank or 1/4 at the least. Put 8.3 gallons for 220 miles today. Math works out to 26 mpg. Pretty good for a V6 I think, considering I'm not as nice to it as I should be sometimes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Your Duke should be doing much better.<P>Maybe you should change brands of fuel. I've found my car is thirstier with Arco fuel.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <P>Best of luck.<BR>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||William Federle||10-26-2001||08:21 AM||||I use 93 octane in my 1984 SE. I have a 4<BR>speed std transmission. In the summer when it's above 90, I get 31 - 33 MPG. I've gone as far as 260 miles on a tank. That put the needle in the middle of the yellow crosshatched area on the fuel gage. Then It might take about 8 gallons to fill it. <P>In the winter my mileage goes down with the temperature. I guess I average about 28 - 29<BR>mpg in the winter. I've always been able to get at least 210 or more miles before the gauge gets to the point mentioned above.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Leadfoot||10-26-2001||11:34 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>Sounds like driving technique.  You should be getting 30+ mpg, unless you are driving with a lead foot. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yah! Us Leadfoots weight 235 lbs. so you need more throttle...i.e. gas to accelerate.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Grammar error!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Leadfoot (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||grinthock||10-26-2001||11:54 AM||||Maybe FieroChic88 can get into this one, she got 40 MPG on her 2m4 she was sayin to me||||reg||1
Z||000012||Gold-86SE||10-26-2001||12:14 PM||||<BR>I get nowhere near 30 mpg. I fill my tank between 160-170 miles on my 84 4.10 4-speed.<P>I run the cheap plugs, wires, and fuel.<BR>I have the 215 60 14 tires.<P>I have replaced the fuel filter, coil, tps, air cleaner, plugs and wires were replaced about 8000 miles or 18 months ago.<P>The engine is from an 85, but all the electronics are from 84, the electronics have to be otherwise it doesn't match to the wiring harness.<P>And I never run with a/c, because I removed it.<P>I drive somewhat gentle, but I have never experience 200+ miles on a tank of fuel.<P>what gives?||||reg||1
Z||000013||William Federle||10-26-2001||01:14 PM||||And I was feeling bad because I wasn't getting over 30 MPG!<P>The engine in my car puts out a lot of oil<BR>smoke until it gets really warmed up. Maybe<BR>yours is just in better shape - tighter.<P>Or maybe the pickup in your tank is higher off the bottom than it is in mine, preventing you from using as much of your fuel as you otherwise could.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfiero||10-26-2001||05:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Maybe FieroChic88 can get into this one, she got 40 MPG on her 2m4 she was sayin to me</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got 38 MPG out of an '87 coupe I had a few years ago.  It was a dog as far as looks and performance go, but I could go 400 miles a tank on long trips!  <P>I also had a Formula (Ed Parks now owns it) that regularly got 33 MPG.  On one trip (to a Fiero show, no less) I went 385 miles on a tank.....that was the farthest (furthest?) I have ever gone on a tank of gas in a V-6 Fiero.<P>My daily driver GT barely gets 30 MPG on long trips and I am currently averaging about 25MPG.  <P>Different cars behave differently, even if they are properly tuned, etc.  You can have 2 identical Fieros with identical mileage, etc. that are well tuned and one will get better mileage than the other.  It's a coin toss sometimes and it's possible that one was just built better at the factory than the other (closer tolerances, etc.).  This is just speculation though, so who really knows? <P>Your mileage may vary.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||862M4inCA||10-28-2001||02:47 AM||||I usually keep a pretty close eye on my mileage and every time I go to the gas station to fill up I've got just a touch over 200 miles (all city) and VERY rarely allow it to get below 1/4 tank on the gauge, but I know for a fact that it runs dry just before it hits the red "E" line. If I'm doing freeway driving I can usually get over 300 on a tank. Always run premium since I get VERY bad performance with regular or mid-grade. Not bad mileage for an average 8-9 gallon fillup.....anyone else agree?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Rich86GT||10-28-2001||10:50 AM||||My '86 GT, 2.8 Liter V6, 5 speed manual, gets 21-23 city and 28-32 highway.  It is out of gas just as the needle hits E, and when empty, will only take a 9.6 gallon fillup.<BR>||||reg||1
